{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Thirty-three.</b> But in their enumeration you find only thirty-two. Rather, this is Yocheved, who was born at their entry into the city, as it is written (Numbers 26:59), \"whom she bore to Levi in Egypt\" — her birth was in Egypt but her conception was not in Egypt. This is the position of our Sages (Sotah 12a). \n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] responded and said that this is astonishing: if so, why did Scripture not mention the wonder that was done with her, that she gave birth to Moses when she was one hundred and thirty years old? And why did it mention the matter of Sarah, who was ninety years old? And is this distress not enough for us, that the liturgical poets composed piyyutim for the day of Simchat Torah: \"Yocheved my mother, after me be comforted\" — and behold she was two hundred and fifty years old! And did Aḥijah the Shilonite live so and so many years? It is the way of the Aggadah or the words of an individual. These are his words.\n\nNow, lest he seem wise in his own eyes in contradicting the words of our Sages, I must answer him. And I say that in any case, in the matter of Yocheved there will be a great wonder beyond the hidden miracles that are the foundation of the Torah, for she was the daughter of Levi himself, not merely traced back to him, as it is written (Numbers 26:59), \"whom she bore to Levi in Egypt,\" and furthermore it is written (Exodus 6:20), \"Yocheved his aunt.\" And now, if we say that he fathered her in his youth as he fathered all his sons, and her birth was shortly after his descent to Egypt, behold she would be very old at the birth of Moses, according to the number our Sages stated or close to it. And if we say she was born to him after he had dwelt in Egypt many days — and suppose he fathered her fifty-seven years after his descent to Egypt, and he would then be one hundred years old, for at his descent he was forty-three years old — behold there would be two wonders here: that he would be old like Abraham, of whom Scripture said (Genesis 17:17), \"Shall one who is a hundred years old beget?\" and as it is written (Genesis 18:12), \"and my master is old,\" and she would be old at the birth of Moses, seventy-three years old. And if we further delay her birth to the end of Levi's days, behold it would be a greater wonder than that of Abraham.\n\nBut I will tell you something that is true and clear in the Torah: the miracles performed through a prophet who prophesies thus beforehand, or [performed] through an angel sent in the mission of the Name — Scripture mentions them. But those that come about on their own to aid a righteous person or to destroy a wicked person — Scripture does not mention them in the Torah or in the Prophets. And this is \"boiling gold poured into the mouth of this sage\" [i.e., a weighty refutation], from what he answered against our Sages concerning the matter of Pinchas (Numbers 25:12) and other matters in many places. For why should Scripture mention them? All the foundations of the Torah are hidden miracles, and the nation of the Torah has in all their affairs only miracles, not nature and custom. For the promises of the Torah are all signs and wonders: that [a transgressor] will not be cut off and die by nature for coming upon one of the forbidden relations or eating forbidden fat, and the heavens will not be iron in their nature because of our seed in the seventh year — and likewise all the promises of the Torah regarding the good things, and all the success of the righteous through their righteousness, and all the prayers of David our king and all our prayers — are miracles and wonders, except that they do not involve a publicly proclaimed change in the nature of the world, as I have already noted (Ramban on Genesis 17:1) and will further explain with the help of the Name (Exodus 6:2; Leviticus 26:11).\n\nNow, I will give you a faithful witness concerning what I have said. We know that from the time Israel came into the land until the birth of our master David was approximately three hundred and seventy years, and the days divide into four generations: Salmon, Boaz, Obed, and Jesse — and each of them reached ninety-three years. Behold, they were all elders, close to the age of Abraham, and each one begot in the year of his death, contrary to the way of all the world, for the lifespan in their time was not one hundred years. And if one of them had begotten in his youth, according to custom, the others would be far older than Abraham, and the wonder in them would be far greater, for the days of a man in the generation of Abraham were long, and in the days of David they returned to half their length. And perhaps they lived even longer, for it is possible that Salmon had many days when they came into the land. Therefore the men of the Kabbalah — and they are sages of truth — assigned to Obed many days, and it is a hidden miracle done for the father of the monarchy, the son of the righteous woman [Ruth] who came to take refuge under the wings of the Shekhinah. And likewise they mention concerning his mother [Ruth] great length of days.\n\nNow, I have already explained that the wonder in Abraham is not as this sage mentioned and others among the masters of Scripture suppose, for Abraham begot Isaac seventy-five years before his death, and not even two parts of his days had passed over him. And among men in every generation, old age does not come upon them until three parts of their days have passed, as the physicians reckon childhood, youth, manhood, and old age. And in these generations, in which the days are about seventy years, the physicians would not reckon him old until after sixty. Furthermore, Abraham begot many sons forty years after the birth of Isaac, and the wonder would be multiplied manifold. And if we say that the El restored him to the days of his youth, the difficulty upon him [Ibn Ezra] would be that Scripture did not mention this great wonder, which is an open and public miracle contrary to nature.\n\nAnd it is well known that men in this generation — some of them beget in their old age up to seventy or eighty years and more, according to how vitality persists in them according to their nature. And women too have no fixed time; as long as they have their menses they give birth. The only astonishment regarding Abraham and Sarah was as I explained there — because they did not beget in their youth, and now they begot, this one from that one, and furthermore there was a wonder in Sarah's flesh, for her menses had ceased as with women, and after that they do not give birth. But behold, if Yocheved's days were like the days of her father, and vitality persisted in her until close to her old age according to the manner of women, it is no wonder if she gave birth at the time our Sages assigned her, because the Elohim desired to redeem Israel through these brothers, and since the appointed end had not yet arrived, He delayed their birth for many days until their mother was old — and nothing is too wondrous for YHWH."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {0 "The phrase וזהו זהב רותח יוצק בפי החכם הזה ('this is boiling gold poured into the mouth of this sage') is a rabbinic idiom for a weighty refutation, rendered hyper-literally here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:46:04.075579626Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}